    "body": "Growing up to an impressive 40 meters tall, the kepuh tree is a fixture of many tropical forest canopies. The kepuh offers several ecological benefits but it is best known for its mythical origins, connecting this tree to both the physical and the spiritual in Balinese tradition.\n\nKepuh trees can be found throughout most of the eastern tropical regions of the Pacific. The wood is not very durable and is only used for some kinds of construction or smaller projects. Its nuts are edible, and its leaves are used to treat various forms of inflammation. As Aini et al.’s scientific review titled “Ethnobotanical Study of Kepuh (Sterculia foetida L.) in Indonesia” reports, kepuh leaves have even been used as a remedy for tuberculosis. In his book Tropical Trees of Indonesia, H.K. Moon describes how the kepuh has historically contributed to traditional art forms. Its seeds have oil used for paints, and the rinds of its fruit have a brown-colored dye often used in dyeing baths for batik textiles.\n\n![S.H. Koorders, Atlas der Baumarten von Java (F. P. W. M. Trap, Leiden, 1915), 416.](/images/writeups/species/kepuh/fig-01.png)\n\n_Its relevance goes beyond the material world, as its origins are often narrated in relation to Dewa Siwa and Dewi Durga, the most prominent couple in the Hindu cosmology, indexing universal duality. Kepuh is associated with Siwa, and thus refers to the male form of the tree, while kepah is his wife, Durga, and therefore refers to the female form of the tree. This mythical history places the kepuh at the heart of Bali’s religious tradition and helps to explain its intense sacrality._\n\n_Several locals shared that because of the kepuh’s spiritual weight, the only maintenance it receives is gentle cleaning or sweeping. Rarely would it be pruned. The kepuh can live for hundreds of years, which further cements it as a fixture of its community. One village official described how its roots extended under the asphalt to far-away towns, a perspective of the kepuh’s power and entrenchment in the environment despite urban development that testifies to its spiritual significance._\n\n_The kepuh is also ecologically important in Bali, and the same official emphasized that without its water-holding strength, the soil of the local Setra Badung would risk being washed away. It is also known for providing shade and shelter. The combination of the kepuh’s deep connection to religious tradition and its support of the local environment makes it one of the most sacred and integral trees in Balinese culture._"
